\section{The shape}{

\code{Distance} accepts one table carrying both the sampling units and the
detections, keyed by \code{Sample.Label}:\tabular{ll}{
   column \tab from \cr
   \code{Region.Label} \tab \code{region}, or the \code{STRATUM} column \cr
   \code{Area} \tab the \code{area} argument \cr
   \code{Sample.Label} \tab \code{seg_id} \cr
   \code{Effort} \tab \code{seg_eff} \cr
   \code{object} \tab one id per detection \cr
   \code{distance} \tab the resolved right-angle distance \cr
   \code{distbegin}, \code{distend} \tab interval bounds, for \code{STRIP}-derived rows \cr
   \code{size} \tab group size \cr
}


\strong{Every segment appears, including those with no detections}, carrying a
missing \code{distance}. This is how the flatfile records effort that produced no
sightings. Dropping those rows would remove effort from the denominator and
inflate density.
}

\section{Truncation applies to two things}{

\code{truncation} drops detections beyond the given distance, and leaves their
segment in place with its effort intact. The effort was searched whether or
not anything was seen within the truncation distance. Removing the segment
too would remove effort from the denominator and bias density upward.

The number of truncated detections is reported. The value must match the
truncation passed to \code{ds()}; otherwise the model is fitted to one set of
detections and abundance is scaled by another.
}

\section{Binned distances cannot be mixed with exact ones}{

\code{STRIP} gives an interval, not a point, so those rows carry \code{distbegin} and
\code{distend} and no \code{distance}. A single \code{ds()} call cannot fit both, because
the likelihoods differ. A table containing both spans a survey era boundary.
\code{mixed = "error"} (the default) refuses it; \code{mixed = "warn"} lets it through
for inspection. Split on \code{distance_source} and fit each era separately.

The top bin of every \code{STRIP} scheme is open-ended (\verb{>1}, \verb{>2}, \verb{>4} nmi), so
\code{distend} is infinite and no detection function can be fitted to it. Those
rows are dropped, and counted in the report.
}

\section{Circling detections}{

Excluded by default. Under the default
\code{segment_survey(circling_distance = "with_group")} there are none to exclude:
the animals were added to the on-effort group they were counted with, which
is already a row here. Under \code{"break_off"} a circling detection's \code{distance}
is either a perpendicular distance from a position fixed minutes after the
detection (\code{distance_source == "circling"}), or, with no exact position, a
great-circle distance from the break-off that is not perpendicular at all
(\code{"break_off"}). Neither is measured at the moment of detection.
\code{include_circling = TRUE} keeps them; state that choice in the methods.

This is independent of whether circling sightings count towards abundance in
a density surface model, which is controlled by
\code{segment_survey(circling = )}. Excluding them here does not remove them from
\code{segs$sightings} or \code{segs$detections}. A surface fitted from this flatfile
alone will miss them; build its observation table from the detections
instead.
}

\section{This assumes g(0) = 1, and it almost certainly is not}{

Nothing in this table corrects for animals that were submerged when the
aircraft passed, or that surfaced and were missed. A detection function
fitted to it treats detection on the track-line as certain. For a
deep-diving species this biases density low, by a large amount.

\code{distsamp} does not estimate \code{g(0)}. It cannot be estimated from a standard
NARWC extract, which records neither dive data nor the double-observer
structure mark-recapture needs. Apply a correction from external sources at
the abundance step, propagate its standard error, and state which components
it covers.
}

\examples{
path <- system.file("extdata", "narwc-example.csv", package = "distsamp")
segs <- segment_survey(read_narwc(path), seg_length = 5, seed = 1)

# Area is required and is yours to supply - it scales abundance directly
flat <- detection_data(segs, area = 5811)
head(flat)

# Segments with no detections are kept, carrying their effort
sum(is.na(flat$distance))

# Truncation drops detections but never the effort that searched for them
nrow(detection_data(segs, area = 5811, truncation = 500))

# With a detection covariate
head(detection_data(segs, area = 5811, covariates = "wt_beaufort"))

}
